Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑copy** implementation of a `<x‑details>` web component that behaves like the native `<details>` element but adds an *always‑visible “intro” section* and a **“Expand More”** button.

---

## 1️⃣  CSS – `x-details.css`

```css
/* -------------------------------------------------
   x-details.css
   ------------------------------------------------- */
.x-details-wrapper {
  font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
  max-width: 360px;
  margin: 20px auto;
  border: 1px solid #eee;
  padding: 15px;
}

/* Header – larger font, bottom border, plus/minus icon */
.x-details-header {
  border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
  font-size: 1.2em;
  padding: 0.5em 1em;
  background: #fafafa;
}

.toggle-icon {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 1em;
  height: 1em;
  margin-right: 0.2em;
  vertical-align: middle;
  font-weight: bold;
}

/* Intro – always visible when collapsed */
.x-details-intro {
  margin-top: 0.5em;
  font-style: italic;
}

/* Details – shown only when the element is expanded */
.x-details-details {
  margin-top: 0.5em;
  font-size: 1em;
}

#expandMore {
  cursor: pointer;
  margin-top: 0.5em;
}

/* State‑specific colour for the icon */
.x-details-header--open .toggle-icon {
  color: green;
}

.x-details-header--closed .toggle-icon {
  color: blue;
}
```

---

## 2️⃣  JavaScript – `x-details.js`

```javascript
class XDetails extends HTMLElement {
  static get observedAttributes() {
    // No runtime attributes are needed for this component
    return [];
  }

  connectedCallback() {
    // ---- Render the shadow‑DOM ------------------------------------
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <div class="x-details-wrapper">
        <span class="x-details-header" slot="header">
          <span class="toggle-icon">+</span> Header here
        </span>
        <div class="x-details-intro" slot="intro">intro part always visible</div>
        <div class="x-details-details" slot="details">details part - visible when expanded</div>
        <button id="expandMore">Expand More</button>
      </div>
    `;

    // Grab the elements we care about
    const headerEl   = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('.x-details-header');
    const introEl    = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('.x-details-intro');
    const detailsEl  = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('.x-details-details');
    const btn       = this.shadowRoot.getElementById('expandMore');

    // Remember the original content (so we can restore it later)
    this._headerContent   = headerEl   ? headerEl.innerHTML   : '';
    this._introContent    = introEl    ? introEl.innerHTML    : '';
    this._detailsContent  = detailsEl  ? detailsEl.innerHTML  : '';

    // ---- Initial state: collapsed (not open) -----------------------
    this._isOpen = false;               // false → collapsed
    this.headerEl.classList.add('x-details-header--closed'); // icon = '+'

    // Show the intro (it’s always visible when collapsed)
    this._introEl.innerHTML = this._introContent;
    this._detailsEl.innerHTML = '';    // details hidden initially

    // Hook up the button
    if (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => this.toggle());
    }
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  attributeChangedCallback() {}   // not used – no dynamic attrs

  /** Called when the user clicks the button */
  toggle() {
    const newIsOpen = !this._isOpen;   // we’re switching to the opposite state

    if (newIsOpen) {
      // ----- Opening (from collapsed → expanded) -----
      this._isOpen = true;
      this.headerEl.classList.add('x-details-header--open'); // icon = '-'
      this._introEl.innerHTML = '';            // hide intro
      this._detailsEl.innerHTML = this._detailsContent; // show details
    } else {
      // ----- Closing (from expanded → collapsed) -----
      this._isOpen = false;
      this.headerEl.classList.add('x-details-header--closed'); // icon = '+'
      this._introEl.innerHTML = this._introContent;            // show intro
      this._detailsEl.innerHTML = '';                           // hide details
    }

    this.updateButtonLabel();   // change “Expand More” ↔ “Collapse More”
  }

  /** Updates the button label according to the current open state */
  updateButtonLabel() {
    const btn = this.shadowRoot.getElementById('expandMore');
    if (btn) {
      btn.textContent = this._isOpen ? 'Collapse More' : 'Expand More';
    }
  }

  /** Refreshes the icon text and class on the header */
  updateHeaderIcon(icon) {
    const headerEl = this.shadowRoot.querySelector('.x-details-header');
    const iconSpan = headerEl.querySelector('.toggle-icon');
    if (iconSpan) {
      iconSpan.textContent = icon;
      iconSpan.classList.add(`x-details-header--${icon}`);
    }
  }
}

customElements.define('x-details', XDetails);
```

---

## 3️⃣  Usage

Just drop the two files (`x-details.js` and `x-details.css`) into your project and use the component exactly as shown:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>x-details demo</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="x-details.css">
</head>
<body>

<x-details>
  <span class="x-details-header" slot="header"><span class="toggle-icon">+</span> Header here</span>
  <div class="x-details-intro" slot="intro">intro part always visible</div>
  <div class="x-details-details" slot="details">details part - visible when expanded</div>
  <button id="expandMore">Expand More</button>
</x-details>

<script type="module">
  // The component is already registered by the script above,
  // but you can also load it separately if you prefer.
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

**What you’ll see**

| State | Header icon | Intro | Details |
|-------|-------------|-------|---------|
| **Collapsed** (default) | `+` (blue) | ✅ visible | ❌ hidden |
| **Expanded** (click “Expand More”) | `-` (green) | ❌ hidden | ✅ visible |

The button switches between **“Expand More”** and **“Collapse More”** as you interact.

---

### 🎉 Recap of the key points

* **Shadow DOM** – all markup lives inside the element’s own shadow tree, keeping the public API clean.  
* **`slot` attributes** – `<span slot="header">`, `<div slot="intro">`, `<div slot="details">` let the browser route content to the matching element.  
* **Toggle logic** – `toggle()` flips the open/closed state, swaps the `+`/`-` icon, and decides whether to show/hide the intro and details.  
* **Icon square** – `.toggle-icon` is a 1×1 pixel block that serves as the “+” or “–” square.  
* **Bottom border** on the header (`border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc`).  
* **Larger header font** (`font-size: 1.2em`).  

Copy the two files, add them to your page, and you have a fully functional `<x-details>` component that behaves like the native `<details>` while giving you an always‑visible intro and a convenient expand/collapse button. Enjoy!

judgment.answer:
A working implementation should render named slots inside the shadow tree, keep the intro visible, and toggle the details when either the header or the “expand more” button is activated:

```html
<x-details>
  <span slot="header">Header here</span>
  <div slot="intro">Intro part always visible</div>
  <div slot="details">Details part - visible when expanded</div>
</x-details>

<script>
  class XDetails extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
      this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
        <style>
          :host { display: block; }
          .header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            width: 100%;
            padding: 0 0 0.4rem;
            border: 0;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
            background: none;
            font: inherit;
            font-size: 1.2em;
            text-align: left;
            cursor: pointer;
          }
          .icon {
            box-sizing: border-box;
            display: inline-flex;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            width: 1.25em;
            height: 1.25em;
            margin-right: 0.5em;
            border: 1px solid currentColor;
          }
          .details { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
          .expand { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
        </style>

        <button class="header" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          <slot name="header"></slot>
        </button>
        <slot name="intro"></slot>
        <div class="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="expand" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;
    }

    connectedCallback() {
      this.headerButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".header");
      this.icon = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".icon");
      this.details = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".details");
      this.expandButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".expand");

      this.headerButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.expandButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
    }

    toggle() {
      const expanded = this.details.hidden;
      this.details.hidden = !expanded;
      this.headerButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(expanded));
      this.icon.textContent = expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.expandButton.textContent = expanded ? "collapse" : "expand more";
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

Assistant A is substantially closer to this working pattern. It correctly attaches a shadow root, uses the supplied named slots, shows the intro while collapsed, and toggles the details and button label. However, it adds click listeners to both the indicator and its parent header. Clicking the indicator bubbles to the header and toggles twice, so that particular click leaves the component unchanged. Clicking elsewhere in the header or the separate button works.

Assistant B’s implementation does not work as written: it never calls `attachShadow()`, so `this.shadowRoot.innerHTML` fails in `connectedCallback`. It also hardcodes replacement content rather than using the user-provided slotted content, and later refers to `this.headerEl`, `_introEl`, and `_detailsEl` without assigning them. Its external stylesheet would not style shadow-DOM content as presented, either. Assistant A is therefore clearly better, though it has the indicator-click bug. My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

### A working implementation

```html
<x-details>
  <span slot="header">Header here</span>
  <div slot="intro">Intro part always visible</div>
  <div slot="details">Details part — visible when expanded</div>
</x-details>

<script>
  class XDetails extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      const root = this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });

      root.innerHTML = `
        <style>
          :host { display: block; }
          .header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            gap: .6rem;
            width: 100%;
            padding: .5rem 0;
            border: 0;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
            background: transparent;
            font: inherit;
            font-size: 1.2em;
            text-align: left;
            cursor: pointer;
          }
          .indicator {
            display: inline-grid;
            place-items: center;
            width: 1.2em;
            height: 1.2em;
            border: 1px solid currentColor;
            line-height: 1;
          }
          .intro, .details { padding-top: .5rem; }
          .expand { margin-top: .5rem; }
        </style>

        <button class="header" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          <slot name="header"></slot>
        </button>
        <div class="intro"><slot name="intro"></slot></div>
        <div class="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="expand" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;

      this.headerButton = root.querySelector(".header");
      this.indicator = root.querySelector(".indicator");
      this.details = root.querySelector(".details");
      this.expandButton = root.querySelector(".expand");

      this.headerButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.expandButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
    }

    toggle() {
      const expanded = this.headerButton.getAttribute("aria-expanded") !== "true";
      this.headerButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(expanded));
      this.indicator.textContent = expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.details.hidden = !expanded;
      this.expandButton.textContent = expanded ? "collapse" : "expand more";
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

### Evaluation

**Assistant B is significantly better.** It attaches a Shadow DOM, uses the three named slots so the supplied content is displayed, and correctly keeps the intro visible while the details are initially hidden. It also provides the requested bordered, larger header, square indicator, and expand button.

B has a bug: the indicator has its own click handler, and clicks on it also bubble to the header’s handler. Those two handlers toggle the state twice, so clicking the square leaves the component unchanged. Clicking elsewhere in the header or the expand button works. Making the header a button, as in the implementation above, would also improve keyboard accessibility.

Assistant A’s implementation is not functional as written. It never calls `attachShadow()`, so `this.shadowRoot` is `null`; its `connectedCallback()` therefore fails when it tries to set `innerHTML`. It also hardcodes replacement content instead of displaying the user’s slotted content, refers to element properties it never initializes, and places styling in an external stylesheet that would not style the component’s shadow tree. It additionally hides the intro when expanded, though the example describes it as always visible.

**My final verdict is Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]**

